WEBVTT

00:00:00.935 --> 00:00:08.957
This is the full flow, not a mock-up, and it is explained step
by step. Four containers talk over real network calls: the

00:00:08.957 --> 00:00:17.244
payment bridge I built, a database, a simulator of a hotel P M
S in the style of the Oracle Hospitality Integration Platform,

00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:22.681
and a simulator of a card processor. Everything is synthetic.
Here is the picture.

00:00:23.286 --> 00:00:31.092
A booking reaches the bridge as an event. The bridge applies a
payment rule and creates a payment link. The guest pays, and

00:00:31.092 --> 00:00:38.707
the card is tokenised the moment it arrives: only a token
comes back, and only ciphertext is stored. The bridge asks the

00:00:38.707 --> 00:00:46.005
processor to authorise and capture, then posts the payment to
the guest's folio in the P M S. Every step carries an

00:00:46.005 --> 00:00:53.683
idempotency key, and every failure ends in one of two places:
posted, or kept in a dead letter queue with the reason. Now

00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:56.539
let me show it, and then break it on purpose.

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:05.476
First, the happy path. The P M S front desk announces a new
reservation and pushes a booking event to the bridge. The

00:01:05.476 --> 00:01:13.118
bridge answers with the payment rule: advance purchase, the
full amount within twenty four hours. Note the business date

00:01:13.118 --> 00:01:18.022
at the top. In a real property it moves only at night audit,
not at midnight.

00:01:19.587 --> 00:01:27.055
In the bridge, a payment request now exists. The guest
tokenises a test card and pays. The card number is encrypted

00:01:27.055 --> 00:01:29.912
on arrival, and the guest sees only a token.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:38.910
On the processor side there is an authorisation and a capture,
and only the last four digits of the card. The processor

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.360
deduplicates on an idempotency key, so a repeat can never
charge twice.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:52.287
And at the front desk, the payment is on the guest's folio:
the reservation, folio window one, a payment code, a cashier,

00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:59.063
eight hundred dollars, our reference and the processor's
reference. It is posted as a payment, not as a charge.

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:07.625
The bridge also tells the hotel's own systems what happened.
Payment captured, and payment posted, each arrive as a signed

00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:15.381
webhook. The receiving side checks the signature before it
trusts the message, and every event has an id, so a repeat is

00:02:15.381 --> 00:02:17.967
ignored. The card number is never in it.

00:02:18.612 --> 00:02:26.474
Edge case one: the same booking event arrives again after the
guest has paid. The bridge does not create a new request, and

00:02:26.474 --> 00:02:33.633
does not ask for money twice. It flags the booking for a
refund or balance review, because a changed total after

00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:35.039
payment needs a human.

00:02:35.643 --> 00:02:43.280
Edge case two: a lost reply. This P M S does not deduplicate,
like the real world. I make it record the next payment and

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:50.916
then answer too late. The bridge times out, but before it
retries it looks for its own reference, finds the payment, and

00:02:50.916 --> 00:02:54.480
does not post twice. One new line on the folio, not two.

00:02:55.143 --> 00:03:02.741
Edge case three: the P M S goes down after the card is
charged. The bridge says so plainly and keeps the payment in a

00:03:02.741 --> 00:03:10.599
dead letter queue with the reason. The money is not lost, and
it is not hidden. When the P M S is back, one click replays

00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:12.807
it, and it is posted exactly once.

00:03:13.778 --> 00:03:21.082
Edge case four: the processor approves the authorisation, then
declines the capture. The bridge does not leave the hold on

00:03:21.082 --> 00:03:28.146
the card. It releases it, and you can see the void in the
processor ledger. The payment link goes back to open, so the

00:03:28.146 --> 00:03:30.780
guest can try again, and this time it works.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:39.548
Edge case five: night audit runs and the business date moves,
and I also expire the access token. The next payment makes the

00:03:39.548 --> 00:03:47.339
processor capture the money and then go quiet. The bridge
fetches a new token, reads the new business date from the P M

00:03:47.339 --> 00:03:51.333
S, retries with the same key, and the processor charges once.

00:03:54.093 --> 00:04:01.225
At the end of the day, finance reconciles. The bridge
downloads the processor's own settlement file and matches

00:04:01.225 --> 00:04:08.615
every capture against what was posted. Five captures, five
matches. Now a messy statement: one capture missing, one

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:13.691
settled short, one we never made. Each exception has a name
finance can act on.

00:04:14.321 --> 00:04:21.204
The operator log shows all of this with the card number
masked. A mask catches mistakes, but the code never logs the

00:04:21.204 --> 00:04:26.011
number in the first place, and the vault view shows
ciphertext, never the number.

00:04:26.658 --> 00:04:33.188
How do I know the other cases hold? Nearly four hundred
automated tests, and a coverage report above ninety nine

00:04:33.188 --> 00:04:40.184
percent of lines. The important tests were checked the hard
way: I put each bug back on purpose, thirty six of them, and

00:04:40.184 --> 00:04:47.355
every one made its test fail. A page in the repository lists
every edge case and the test that fails if it breaks, and also

00:04:47.355 --> 00:04:48.521
what is not covered.

00:04:49.224 --> 00:04:56.696
How it should work in production. The simulators become real
adapters: Oracle's own sandbox for the P M S, and the

00:04:56.696 --> 00:05:04.496
acquirer's checkout for the processor, with three D secure and
hosted card entry so a guest's number never reaches this

00:05:04.496 --> 00:05:12.492
service. Authentication is already in: OAuth2 tokens or hashed
API keys, each limited to what that client may do. Still to

00:05:12.492 --> 00:05:20.620
add: tenants for many operators and properties, a sweeper for
crashes between steps, chargeback cases with scheme deadlines,

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.504
and key management proven in a real account.

00:05:24.197 --> 00:05:32.418
That is the full flow: idempotent charges, a repeated event, a
lost reply, an outage and a replay, a released hold, a token

00:05:32.418 --> 00:05:40.371
refresh, and reconciliation, against separate systems. The P M
S here is a simulator I wrote in the shape of the Oracle

00:05:40.371 --> 00:05:48.391
Hospitality Integration Platform. It is not Oracle software,
and the real thing needs Oracle's own sandbox, which is the

00:05:48.391 --> 00:05:55.141
next step. The code, the tests and the log of what the agents
got wrong are in the public repository.
